/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - With v(t)=Vm sin(ωt+θv) and i(t)=Im sin(ωt+θi), instantaneous power is p(t)=v(t)i(t).
   - p(t) = Vrms Irms cos φ − Vrms Irms cos(2ωt + θv + θi)
   - Vm, Im are peak volts and amps; Vrms=Vm/√2 and Irms=Im/√2; ω=2πf; φ=θv−θi is the impedance angle.
   - The second term has frequency 2f, so power pulses twice per supply cycle, and its average over a cycle is zero.
   - Reactive power is Q=Vrms Irms sinφ, in volt-ampere reactive (VAR).
   - P is real power in W; Q is reactive power in VAR; |S| is apparent power in VA; φ is the power-factor angle; j is the imaginary unit.
   - The power triangle is a right triangle with P on the adjacent side, Q on the opposite side and |S| on the hypotenuse.
   - Power factor is cosφ=P/|S|, the cosine of the angle between voltage and current; for a series circuit it also equals R/|Z|.
*/
