/* book exam traps: Using peak values in V I: average power uses RMS. For a sine, Vrms=Vm/√2, so P=Vrms Irms cosφ=(Vm Im/2)cosφ. | Adding apparent powers: 100 VA at 0.6 pf plus 100 VA at 0.8 pf is not 200 VA. Add P and Q separately, then |S|=√(P²+Q²). | Getting the sign of Q wrong: inductive lagging has Q positive; capacitive leading has Q negative. A capacitor's QC is negative in the sum Sload+SC. | Forgetting that power-factor correction leaves P unchanged: the capacitor changes Q and line current, not the motor's watts. | Mixing units: W for P, VAR for Q, VA for |S|. Do not write VAR for apparent power or W for reactive power. */
