/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A three-phase source has three voltages of the same RMS value and frequency.
   - VL = √3 Vph and IL = Iph (star); VL = Vph and IL = √3 Iph (delta)
   - Vph is the RMS phase voltage across one branch; VL is the RMS line-to-line voltage; Iph is the RMS current inside one phase branch; IL is the RMS current in one line conductor.
   - In a star (wye) connection each phase sits between one line and the neutral, so Vph is line-to-neutral and VL is line-to-line.
   - For R-Y-B sequence the star line voltage VRY leads VR by 30° (see the figure), and in delta the line current IR lags the phase current IRY by 30°.
   - A balanced load has the same Z in every phase, so the three currents are equal and 120° apart; their sum, the neutral current of a four-wire star, is zero.
   - A balanced delta load can also be replaced by an equivalent star with Zstar = Zdelta/3 and solved the same way.
   - An unbalanced load has different Z in the three phases, or different phase angles.
   - VN′N = (VRYR + VYYY + VBYB) / (YR + YY + YB)
   - VN′N is the shift of the load star point N′ from the source neutral N; VR, VY, VB are source phase voltages; YR = 1/ZR, YY = 1/ZY, YB = 1/ZB are phase admittances.
*/
