/* book Worked example 2: Q: A 400 V, 50 Hz, R-Y-B (positive sequence) four-wire supply feeds a star-connected resistive load: ZR = 10 Ω, ZY = 20 Ω, ZB = 20 Ω. (a) Find the line currents and the neutral current. (b) The neutral wire breaks. Find the shift of the load star point and the new load phase voltages and currents.
   1. (a) Phase voltages, VR as reference: Vph = 400/√3 = 230.9 V, so VR = 230.9∠0°, VY = 230.9∠−120°, VB = 230.9∠120° V.
   2. With the neutral in place each phase sees its own supply voltage: IR = 230.9/10 = 23.09∠0° A, IY = 230.9/20 = 11.55∠−120° A, IB = 11.55∠120° A.
   3. Neutral current: IN = IR + IY + IB. The Y and B currents are equal and 120° apart, so their sum is 11.55∠180°. IN = 23.09 − 11.55 = 11.55∠0° A.
   4. (b) Without the neutral, use the star-point formula with YR = 0.1 S, YY = YB = 0.05 S: VN′N = (230.9 × 0.1 + 230.9∠−120° × 0.05 + 230.9∠120° × 0.05)/0.2. The two 0.05 S terms add to 11.55∠180°, so VN′N = (23.09 − 11.55)/0.2 = 57.7∠0° V.
   5. New load phase voltages: VR − VN′N = 230.9 − 57.7 = 173.2 V; VY − VN′N = (−115.5 − j200) − 57.7 = −173.2 − j200 V, that is 264.6∠−130.9° V; by symmetry the B phase has 264.6∠130.9° V.
   6. New currents: IR = 173.2/10 = 17.32 A; IY = 264.6/20 = 13.23∠−130.9° A = −8.66 − j10.0 A; IB = −8.66 + j10.0 A. Check: 17.32 − 8.66 − 8.66 = 0 and +j10 − j10 = 0, so the sum is zero as KCL needs.
   Answer: (a) IR = 23.09 A, IY = IB = 11.55 A, IN = 11.55∠0° A. (b) The star point shifts by 57.7 V; the heavily loaded R phase drops to 173.2 V while Y and B rise to 264.6 V, which is why a broken neutral burns out appliances on the lightly loaded phases. Total power falls from 10.67 kW to 10.0 kW. */
